      // [src_wegener_hofbauer2005, src_wegener_demhardt2005, src_wegener_own_book1929, src_wegener_lake1923 | 1912-1929 | scholars + self + adversarial critique] He advanced a major hypothesis while openly leaving its driving mechanism unresolved. In 1912 he wrote that he could not yet say what forces tore the first crust open and that the time seemed 'not ripe' for an analysis of causes (Hofbauer quoting the 1912 paper; Demhardt records the tide and 'accidental currents' suggestions); in his 1929 preface he says the truth can only be found by combining all earth sciences and that any new discovery in any science may modify the result (own preface). Critics used the gap: Jeffreys in 1923 called the proposed causes 'ridiculously inadequate' (Lake discussion). Two distinct instances across seventeen years from independent readers plus his own framing; dual_edged because the open mechanism became the main objection.
      ambiguity_tolerance: [68, 0.55, "s", "D"],
      // [src_wegener_hofbauer2005, src_wegener_own_book1929, src_wegener_lake1923 | 1912-1929 | scholar + self + adversarial critique] Genuinely mixed, so scored near the middle at low confidence. For: he reworked the book for the 1920, 1922 and 1929 editions as the literature grew, credits earlier authors by name (Taylor, Pickering, Mantovani and others), and in the 1929 preface admits that he himself once wrote, 'in a weak hour', that geophysics alone could decide the matter, contrary to his later stance. Against: Hofbauer notes that he kept his estimate of 14-28 m per year of drift in Greenland until 1929 although there were well-founded views that the Atlantic was far older there, paid only superficial attention to the mantle-convection idea of Holmes, and in his later efforts to win acceptance added no fundamentally new arguments; in 1923 Debenham said he 'changes his ground' (Lake discussion, adversarial). One scholarly judgement plus self and adversarial remarks, no single decisive episode; not stronger either way.
      belief_updating: [45, 0.4, "i", "D"],
